From fbd34a3c31a2db98889811650b20aec3cbcc06ac Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kebab bring-up <local@invalid>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 13:33:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] kebab: move front IMX471 to CCI1 master 1, where the vendor
 puts it

The vendor devicetree for this exact board states the front camera's CCI
master twice, once for the sensor and once for its EEPROM
(kona-oem-camera-kebab_t0.dtsi, under &cam_cci1):

  eeprom_front_0: qcom,eeprom@2    cci-master = <1>   /* Front cam: IMX471 */
  qcom,cam-sensor@2                cci-master = <1>   /* Front cam: IMX471 */
  eeprom_rear_2:  qcom,eeprom@3    cci-master = <0>   /* Micro: GC5035   */
  qcom,cam-sensor@3                cci-master = <0>   /* Micro: GC5035   */

Master 0 of CCI1 is the GC5035 macro camera, not the front camera. A live
scan of both child buses agrees with that split:

  i2c-23  (cci1 master 0)  ->  0x50, 0x58
  i2c-24  (cci1 master 1)  ->  0x54, 0x5c

This matters for more than just addressing. The EEPROM at 0x50/0x58 that
has been ACKing next to our sensor node since 0059, and that every
previous session cited as proof that "the bus is electrically alive, so
the silent device must be a dead sensor", belongs to the GC5035. It was
never the IMX471's EEPROM. The front module's own EEPROM is at 0x54/0x5c
on master 1, a bus we have not had the sensor on.

Patch 0059 put the sensor on master 0; 0073 moved it to master 1 and 0074
moved it back. Neither of the two arguments for master 0 holds up:

 - 0073's -ENXIO is void. It ran on 2026-09-17, before 0077, so VANA was
   still inverted by 0060 and the sensor's analog rail was switched off
   for the entire transaction. Like the 0070/0075 scans and the 0066 VDIG
   result, it measured an unpowered part.

 - The stock module identifier "imx471_p24c64e" implies a 24C64-family
   EEPROM somewhere in 0x50-0x57, and that was read as pointing at the
   0x50 on master 0. It does not discriminate: 0x54 on master 1 is just
   as much inside that range, and the vendor DT says master 1 explicitly.

Move the node to cci1_i2c1 and carry the 400kHz fast-mode rate with it.
The client will enumerate as 24-001a rather than 23-001a.
---
 .../boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts    | 37 +++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 25 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
index eba02a274..77ba5549e 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm8250-oneplus-kebab.dts
@@ -2032,20 +2032,33 @@ csiphy4_front_ep: endpoint {
 	};
 };
 
-&cci1_i2c0 {
+&cci1_i2c1 {
 	/*
-	 * Fast-mode (400kHz), not the 1MHz Fm+ that sm8250.dtsi defaults every
-	 * CCI child bus to. Sony IMX-family sensors are specified for 400kHz
-	 * I2C; the 24C64-style module EEPROM sharing this bus is an Fm+ part,
-	 * which is why it kept ACKing at 1MHz while the sensor never did.
+	 * The front IMX471 lives on CCI1 master 1, not master 0. The vendor
+	 * devicetree for this board says so twice, for the sensor and for its
+	 * own EEPROM (kona-oem-camera-kebab_t0.dtsi):
 	 *
-	 * Note this was never actually tested at a legal rate before: prior to
-	 * patch 0072 the CCI v2 timing table was being fed 19.2MHz instead of
-	 * the 37.5MHz it is calibrated for, so a requested 1MHz came out at
-	 * roughly 1MHz * 19.2/37.5 ~= 512kHz -- still over spec. 0072 then
-	 * corrected the timing reference and thereby raised real SCL to a true
-	 * 1MHz, further over spec. Both full-bus scans (0070, 0075) ran under
-	 * one or the other of those conditions.
+	 *   eeprom_front_0: qcom,eeprom@2   cci-master = <1>  / Front cam: IMX471
+	 *   qcom,cam-sensor@2               cci-master = <1>  / Front cam: IMX471
+	 *   eeprom_rear_2:  qcom,eeprom@3   cci-master = <0>  / Micro: GC5035
+	 *   qcom,cam-sensor@3               cci-master = <0>  / Micro: GC5035
+	 *
+	 * Master 0 belongs to the GC5035 macro camera. A live scan of both
+	 * buses agrees: i2c-23 (master 0) answers at 0x50/0x58, i2c-24
+	 * (master 1) answers at 0x54/0x5c. The EEPROM that has been ACKing
+	 * next to our sensor node all along, and which was read as evidence
+	 * that "the bus is alive so the sensor must be dead", is the GC5035's.
+	 *
+	 * Patch 0059 originally put the sensor on master 0, and 0073 tried
+	 * master 1 and was reverted by 0074. But 0073 ran before 0077, i.e.
+	 * while VANA was inverted and the sensor was unpowered for the whole
+	 * transaction, so its NACK carried no information. The other argument
+	 * for master 0 -- that the stock module id "imx471_p24c64e" implies a
+	 * 24C64 part in 0x50-0x57 -- does not discriminate either, because
+	 * 0x54 on master 1 is equally inside that range.
+	 *
+	 * Retest master 1 with correct VANA polarity, the vendor power
+	 * sequence and an in-spec bus rate.
 	 */
 	clock-frequency = <400000>;
 
-- 
2.55.0

